teratively as new sources and use cases arrive."]],"related":["/ai-architect/","/healthcare-data-architecture/","/fractional-cto/"]}-->
<section class="block">
<h2>Signs you need an ontology</h2>
<p>The symptom is rarely described as a semantic problem. It sounds like this:</p>
<ul>
<li>Two dashboards show different revenue numbers and both are "right".</li>
<li>Sales, finance and support each have their own definition of an active customer.</li>
<li>Every new data source takes months to integrate because nobody agrees how it maps.</li>
<li>Your AI assistant answers fluently and gets relationships wrong: the wrong contract, the wrong entity, the wrong version.</li>
<li>A data catalogue exists, but nobody trusts it.</li>
</ul>
<p>Adding another pipeline does not fix any of these. It copies the confusion somewhere new. What fixes it is a shared, written model of meaning. If the terms are muddled, start with my explainer on <a href="/insights/ontology-vs-knowledge-graph-vs-semantic-layer/">ontology vs knowledge graph vs semantic layer</a>.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>The problems I solve</h2>
<h3>One definition of truth</h3>
<p>I run domain workshops with the people who own the business concepts, capture the entities, relationships and rules, and turn them into an ontology that is versioned and reviewed like code.</p>
<h3>Joining data that was never designed to join</h3>
<p>Entity resolution connects the same customer, patient or supplier across CRM, ERP, documents and legacy systems, with clear rules and confidence scores rather than one-off scripts.</p>
<h3>Meaning that reaches every consumer</h3>
<p>A semantic layer exposes the model to BI tools, APIs and applications, so a metric is defined once and used everywhere.</p>
<h3>AI that understands your business</h3>
<p>A knowledge graph gives LLMs structured context. Retrieval can follow relationships, answers can cite facts, and access rules can be enforced at query time. This is the foundation of the GraphRAG systems I build as an <a href="/ai-architect/">AI architect</a>.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>My approach</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Pick the questions first.</strong> Which decisions, reports or AI use cases must the model support? That keeps the ontology useful rather than academic.</li>
<li><strong>Model the core domain.</strong> A small set of well-defined concepts beats an exhaustive taxonomy nobody maintains.</li>
<li><strong>Reuse standards where they exist.</strong> Industry vocabularies save years of argument. In healthcare that means SNOMED CT, LOINC and RxNorm; in other domains, the relevant public schemas.</li>
<li><strong>Map sources and resolve entities.</strong> Source by source, with data quality checks at the boundaries.</li>
<li><strong>Choose the right store.</strong> RDF and OWL with SPARQL where reasoning and standards matter, a property graph such as Neo4j with Cypher where operational speed matters.</li>
<li><strong>Serve it.</strong> Semantic layer for analytics, APIs for applications, graph retrieval for AI agents.</li>
<li><strong>Govern it lightly.</strong> Ownership, change review and versioning, so the model keeps pace with the business.</li>
</ol>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>What you get</h2>
