<section class="block">
<h2>How an engagement runs</h2>
<p>A first useful ontology usually comes out of three to four weeks of workshops and modelling, often as part of an architecture review. Building the graph, mappings and semantic layer is then a project of a few months, delivered in slices: one domain, one consumer, one measurable win at a time. I work embedded with your data team so the knowledge stays with them.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>See the <a href="/#work">enterprise knowledge graph case study</a> for the shape of a typical engagement.</p></div>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>Common mistakes with ontologies</h2>
<h3>Modelling the whole world</h3>
<p>The most common failure is ambition. A team sets out to model every concept in the enterprise, spends a year in workshops and delivers a beautiful diagram nobody uses. I start with the handful of concepts behind the questions that matter, then grow the model as use cases arrive.</p>
<h3>Leaving the business out</h3>
<p>An ontology written only by engineers encodes engineering assumptions. The people who own the concepts, such as finance for revenue or clinicians for diagnoses, have to agree the definitions. My workshops are short and focused so senior people actually attend.</p>
<h3>No path to production</h3>
<p>A model that lives in a modelling tool and never reaches a pipeline, an API or a dashboard has no effect. Every modelling sprint I run ends with something consumed by a real system.</p>
<h3>Choosing a graph database before a use case</h3>
<p>RDF triple stores and property graphs both work well. Picking one before you know the queries, the reasoning needs and the team's skills leads to awkward workarounds later. I make that choice with the use cases on the table.</p>
<h3>No owner after go-live</h3>
<p>Businesses change, and so must the model. Without named owners and a light change process, the ontology falls out of date and trust disappears. Governance does not have to be heavy, but it has to exist.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>Where knowledge graphs pay off fastest</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Customer 360.</strong> One connected view of accounts, contacts, contracts and support history across systems.</li>
<li><strong>Product and supply data.</strong> Parts, suppliers and compliance attributes that must stay consistent across catalogues.</li>
<li><strong>Clinical and research data.</strong> Conditions, drugs and lab results linked through standard vocabularies.</li>
<li><strong>Enterprise AI assistants.</strong> Grounded answers that cite the facts they depend on.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>Relevant experience</h2>
